Backend
  In your terminal, run:
  cd /Users/jonathanbeltran/service-center/backend
  source venv/bin/activate
  ADMIN_PASSWORD=yourpassword uvicorn main:app --reload
 You should see Uvicorn running on http://127.0.0.1:8000.

Frontend
  The backend serves both pages, so open them through it (opening the files
  directly from disk will not work):
  - http://127.0.0.1:8000/index.html — the worker shift picker
  - http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin.html — the supervisor panel

How scheduling works
  Supervisor panel (admin.html) has four tabs:
  1. Create Schedule: set the period (start and end dates), shift sizes, and
     add workers. This is a draft. It saves as you type, but workers see
     none of it until it is published.
     Marking a worker "Absent" moves them to the bottom of the scheduling
     order, below the lowest priority-point holder; absent workers are
     ordered among themselves by priority points.
     "Can't-Work Shifts" (after the worker list) marks shifts a worker
     absolutely cannot work: they are never scheduled there, by their own
     picks or by autofill. These save right away and workers never see them.
     "Autofill Empty Shifts" (on by default): when workers' picks leave
     required seats empty, each one goes to the worker with the fewest shifts
     that week, lowest ranking breaking ties (absent workers rank lowest).
     Forced seats show as [JB] on the results and in the Excel export.
     Yellow seats are optional and never forced. Once the required seats are
     filled, they are handed out one at a time going down the scheduling
     order: each worker takes their highest-ranked yellow pick still open,
     then the next worker, and so on, in rounds until none are left.
  2. Publish Schedule: shows the empty schedule built from the draft. Click a
     cell to mark that shift as +$1 incentive pay (light green), or twice for
     +$4 (dark green); workers see the same green marking when picking
     shifts. Click a day's name to give that one
     day its own shift sizes, yellows and an optional note (e.g. a holiday);
     other days keep the defaults, and the note goes in the Excel notes row.
     Pressing "Publish Schedule" posts it to staff. Workers can then open
     index.html, pick their initials, and rank shifts for each week. Coming
     back later reloads what they already submitted. Pressing Publish
     Schedule again re-publishes the draft; submissions for weeks no longer
     in the period are deleted.
     "Remove from Staff View" takes the schedule down so workers see nothing.
     The draft and submitted shifts are kept; Publish Schedule posts it again.
  3. Run Schedule: shows who has submitted, runs the algorithm on the
     published schedule, and shows the result. Workers never see it in the
     app; "Download Excel" produces a workbook (one sheet per week, laid out
     from backend/template.xlsx) for the supervisor to share.
  4. History: every time the schedule was run, newest first, with the date
     it ran, the period and whether every seat was filled. Each run can be
     viewed again, downloaded as Excel, or deleted; it keeps the settings it
     ran with, so later changes to the schedule don't alter it. The last 200
     runs are kept.

Live updates
  Both pages check the server every 10 seconds while they are on screen, and
  right away when you switch back to them:
  - the supervisor's Submitted Shifts list picks up new worker submissions;
  - the worker page follows the supervisor: it shows the schedule as soon as
    it is published, and hides it when it is removed from staff view (keeping
    the worker's unsaved picks in case it comes back).

Persistence
  All state (draft, published schedule, submissions, can't-work marks, the
  latest results and run history) is saved to a JSON file. Default:
  backend/store.json. Override with DATA_FILE.
  On Railway, attach a volume and set DATA_FILE=/data/store.json so submissions
  survive redeploys and restarts.
